A||||5||Johnson's85SE||Engine Flush||||1||
Z||000000||Johnson's85SE||05-01-2001||12:53 PM||||What are the pros and cons of doing an engine oil flush? Will this wreck my oil seals causing oil leaks. My engine is high milage, and I would like to clean out all that gunk in her. Will an engine flush reduce some of that ticking? I either have an exhaust leak, or a valve with gunk on it. That sowing machine tick, is most likly which of the above?<BR>Cheers!<P>------------------<BR>If it isn't broke, it's not my car.||||reg||1
Z||000001||84Bill||05-01-2001||12:59 PM||||The best way is just to change the oil on a regular basis. I had a 72 Impalla with a timing chain slap it was quite grungy in there. after 2 years of use I disposed of the car but kept the engine and much to my surprise it was quite clean in there I guess the regular oil changes really made a difference! ||||reg||14
Z||000002||Carrolles||05-01-2001||02:19 PM||||I bought an 85GT with an abused tired motor (although it had only 113000 miles) that clicked, ticked, and stumbled badly.  I used an engine flush when I got the car and followed the directions which said to add flush and let engine run at idle for 20 minutes then change oil and filter.  I figured I didn't have much to lose on this engine.<P>I did so and it quited down some.  No new leaks developed.<P>I did it again about a month later and also decarbonized and added a can of Restore with the new oil.  It quieted down a lot and smoothed out quite a bit.<P>I still have a bad lifter tap at low rpms.  It smoothes out around 2500 rpms and pulls strong.  Still no new leaks.<P>So far it has turned out to be a pretty reliable engine.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||thomas_l||05-01-2001||04:59 PM||||I wouldn't do it. I had a friend with an 86 Cierra 2.8L that was having low oil pressure problems due to crap clogging the pick up tube. With the oil pan off it was clear that the engine was crudded up BAD. I took the intake and valve covers off and spent a lot of time scraping and brushing and washing sludge out with diesel. It then took about a case of oil filters and 12 oil changes (I recycled a case of oil thru a filter to get the big chunks out) before all the soot was gone and it quit losing pressure again. When it was all over I think the flush product washed out the bearings and loosened things up. The motor survived, but it had low (not terminal) oil pressure at idle and a little bearing chatter over 3k rpm.<P>Lesson learned: It was a big waste of time & materials and netted little benefit. If your engine's dirty inside, try not to disturb it with magic cleaning products (basically kerosene or diesel in a can) and keep the oil/filter changes frequent. The time to clean up is when it gets torn down for an overhaul.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tom88gt||05-01-2001||09:01 PM||||I've used motor flush on every used car I've ever bought.  Never had a leak develop after using the stuff.  Even high mileage cars.  I've been told that motor flush is just kerosene but never tried it.||||reg||14
Z||000005||KRMFiero||05-01-2001||09:27 PM||||I always use Engine flush and when i dont have enough money i use a cup of karosean||||reg||1
